From 407f77e7a825e41eecbee3a7d83384c84616ea59 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ws07 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 23:58:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Web client (S1): walks round the palisades that stop
 them; the forecast by the program's clash model
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Connection patch of workstream 07 (player experience), claims 1, 2 and 4.

- frontier/s1walk.mjs: structure.rs `stops`, `watch`, `march_watch`,
  `caravan_watch`, `wear_bps` and `wear`, ported and pinned against vectors
  the rules crate writes (test/fixtures/s1-walk-v1.json).
- The march planner (composer, keep lists, action bar) is given the hexes of
  the palisades that stop the viewer (`blocked`), and the caravan roads
  (send, raid take) are planned with those tiles removed from the passable
  masks, as S1-1E-CONTRACT §2.5 asks of every client. The page planned
  through them, and the program refused the walk (`Path`).
- The S1 march forecast hands the kernel's own builder (`resolve_from_inputs`)
  the destination's Province bytes and a ClashInputs account with the
  viewer's host alone, its troops worn by the towers on its road: the keep's
  garrison, the manned forts and the caravans are in the clash as the
  program has them. The page's builder (screens/report.mjs buildClashArgs)
  had none of them: on a recorded keep clash (uxs1, turn 619) it forecast
  500 left where the chain recorded 488. The herald client keeps the
  Province bytes it decoded.
- The siege offer holds back three refusals of declare_siege the page can
  tell from its records (the declarer's own village shielded, a busy site, a
  stake owed) and names the checks left to the program.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
